<Article headerTimer h1={"Dead hang time by age: what does your result mean?"} subtitle={"Your age alone does not set a required hang time. Most research cited here measures handgrip force; the two timed-hang samples below are small and young. Use your own repeatable baseline and treat training milestones as goals and not population rankings."} crumbs={[{"name":"Times by age"}]} title="Dead Hang Times by Age Chart: Men & Women" description="What is a good dead hang time? No measured norm exists. Young athletes averaged 64 s (men) and 48 s (women) in one study; Attia's 40s target is 90 to 120 s." schema={jsonLd}>
<div class="article-body">
<div class="callout" id="short-answer"><p><strong>Short answer</strong></p><p>A useful starting point is a controlled hold you can repeat without pain. No large study has measured dead hang time across the population and there is no verified average. The main measured sample is 25 recreational athletes aged about 20 (GRIP-6, 2025): men held 64.0 seconds and women 48.4 seconds on average with wide variation.</p></div>
<div class="ng" role="group" aria-label="Reference figures with sources, in three groups">
<div class="ng-group ng-measured" role="group" aria-labelledby="ng-m">
<p class="ng-title" id="ng-m"><span class="ng-mark" aria-hidden="true">&#9679;</span> Measured in small studies</p>
<p class="ng-note">GRIP-6 study (2025): 25 trained recreational athletes aged about 20. A small young sample and not a norm.</p>
<div class="ng-cards">
<div class="ng-card"><div class="ng-value">64.0 s</div><div class="ng-label">Men: mean of three hangs (11 men, SD 35.3 s)</div></div>
<div class="ng-card"><div class="ng-value">48.4 s</div><div class="ng-label">Women: mean of three hangs (14 women, SD 29.1 s)</div></div>
</div>
<p class="ng-source">Source: GRIP-6 study (2025)</p>
</div>
<div class="ng-group ng-target" role="group" aria-labelledby="ng-t">
<p class="ng-title" id="ng-t"><span class="ng-mark" aria-hidden="true">&#9670;</span> A suggested training target</p>
<p class="ng-note">A podcast target for very strong people. It is a goal and not a norm or measured data.</p>
<div class="ng-cards">
<div class="ng-card"><div class="ng-value">At least 120 s</div><div class="ng-label">Attia's target for a very strong 40-year-old man</div></div>
<div class="ng-card"><div class="ng-value">At least 90 s</div><div class="ng-label">Attia's target for a very strong 40-year-old woman</div></div>
</div>
<p class="ng-source">Source: The Drive #329 (Dec 2024)</p>
</div>
<div class="ng-group ng-record" role="group" aria-labelledby="ng-r">
<p class="ng-title" id="ng-r"><span class="ng-mark" aria-hidden="true">&#9642;</span> Record category</p>
<p class="ng-record-line"><span class="ng-value">80:41</span> <span class="ng-label">Guinness record for the dead hang position. Set by Kenta Adachi (Japan) on 18 Nov 2022. A world record and not a benchmark for your own hold.</span></p>
<p class="ng-source">Source: Guinness World Records</p>
</div>
</div>
<p class="bya-updated">Updated 1 November 2026. The GRIP-6 sample is young and trained. Attia's numbers are a podcast target for very strong people. Neither is a population average.</p>
<div class="callout" id="how-long-answer"><p><strong>How long should you dead hang?</strong></p><p>No official target exists. In the one measured sample, men averaged 64.0 seconds and women 48.4 seconds (GRIP-6, 2025, mean age 20.4). Attia's reported target for a very strong 40-year-old is at least 120 seconds for a man and 90 seconds for a woman. Treat 10, 30, 60 and 120 seconds as personal milestones and not cut-offs. For one session, Level 1 is 3 hangs of 15 seconds, set out in <a href="#how-long-per-session">the session section below</a>. If you want to know what hanging may and may not do for you, read <a href="/deadhang-benefits/">the dead hang benefits guide</a>.</p></div>
<section class="yn-slot" aria-label="Your number"><YourNumberCard client:load /><noscript><p>The interactive card needs JavaScript. Compare your hold with the labelled groups below by hand.</p></noscript></section>
<h2 id="average-times">Dead hang times by age: what the chart can tell you</h2>
<p>An age chart is only as useful as the test behind it. The same person can record different hang times on different bars or with different assistance. Keep the testing conditions consistent before interpreting a change.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Age and hang-time interpretation"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Age group</th><th scope="col">What to use</th><th scope="col">What not to infer</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[['Adults under 40','Your own baseline with a consistent grip and bar.','An exact percentile from a generic internet seconds chart.'],['Adults 40 to 59','Comparable holds and a record of assistance used.','A fixed decline in hanging seconds each decade.'],['Adults 60 and over','A controlled, suitable variation and individual advice where needed.','A required unassisted hold or a lifespan prediction.']].map(([age,use,avoid])=><tr><th scope="row">{age}</th><td>{use}</td><td>{avoid}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h3 id="measured-sample">Measured hang times in a published study</h3>
<p>The 2025 study measured 25 trained recreational athletes aged about 20. Men held a passive bar hang for 64 seconds on average and women for 48 seconds. Individual variation was wide. No large population study of dead hang time exists. Treat these as one measured sample and not a norm.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Dead hang times measured in the GRIP-6 study"><table><caption>Passive bar hang time, mean (standard deviation), seconds. Chomiuk et al., GRIP-6 study, 2025, Table 2.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">People</th><th scope="col">Before 6 weeks of training</th><th scope="col">After 6 weeks of training</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Men</th><td>11</td><td>64.0 (35.3)</td><td>69.4 (33.7)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Women</th><td>14</td><td>48.4 (29.1)</td><td>54.3 (28.7)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Whole group</th><td>25</td><td>55.3</td><td>60.9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="visual-block bya-chart"><div class="dg-chart dg-range" role="img" aria-label="Dead hang times in the GRIP-6 study. Mean hang time with one standard deviation either side. Men before training 64.0 seconds, after 69.4. Women before training 48.4 seconds, after 54.3. The bars overlap heavily, so individual times vary widely."><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Men, before</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:53.33%">64.0 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:23.92%;width:58.83%"></i><i class="dg-dot open" style="left:53.33%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Men, after</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:57.83%">69.4 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:29.75%;width:56.17%"></i><i class="dg-dot solid" style="left:57.83%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Women, before</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:40.33%">48.4 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:16.08%;width:48.5%"></i><i class="dg-dot open" style="left:40.33%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Women, after</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:45.25%">54.3 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:21.33%;width:47.83%"></i><i class="dg-dot solid" style="left:45.25%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow dg-raxisrow"><span class="dg-rlabel" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="dg-raxis"><span style="left:0%">0 s</span><span style="left:25%">30 s</span><span style="left:50%">60 s</span><span style="left:75%">90 s</span><span style="left:100%">120 s</span></div></div></div><p class="caption">Dots are means (open before training, filled after). Bars span one standard deviation either side of the mean. GRIP-6 study, 2025.</p></div>
<p>The standard deviation is more than half the mean for both sexes. Individual times in this sample ranged widely. An older and smaller test gives a second reference point (Drury et al. 2004). The test found that 32 moderately trained men averaged 95.4 seconds on a standard bar and 107.6 seconds on a different bar design called the Tri-Bar. That is a male-only sample on two bars. It is not a norm either.</p>
<div class="evidence-note bya-method"><p><strong>How the GRIP-6 hangs were measured.</strong> The grip was overhand at shoulder width with legs off the ground and straight arms. Each person hung passively until they dropped. Three trials were averaged and separated by three minutes of rest. The 25 participants were 11 men and 14 women. All were healthy recreational athletes with a mean age of 20.4. Source: <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/jfmk10040427" rel="noopener">Chomiuk et al. J Funct Morphol Kinesiol 2025;10(4):427</a>. A young trained sample is not a guide to every age. Compare older or untrained hangs with care.</p></div>
<h2 id="male-standards">Men’s times and women’s times</h2>
<p id="female-standards">Published handgrip-force norms differ by age and sex. Those measurements use a dynamometer and report force. Supporting bodyweight from a bar for time involves another task. Applying the force percentiles directly to hang duration would be misleading.</p>
<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0113637" rel="noopener">Dodds et al. explain the instruments and populations in their study</a>. That paper does not provide the age-specific dead-hang seconds previously displayed here.</p>
<h2 id="expert-targets">Expert hang-time targets for men and women</h2>
<p>Peter Attia said on his podcast in December 2024 that a "very strong" 40-year-old man should be able to dead hang for at least two minutes and a "very strong" 40-year-old woman for at least 90 seconds. That is his target for strong people. It is not an average. 11 men averaged 64 seconds and 14 women 48 seconds in the GRIP-6 study. Their mean age was about 20.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Expert targets compared with measured averages"><table><caption>Targets and measured means side by side. They describe different people.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Figure</th><th scope="col">Who</th><th scope="col">What it represents</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">At least 120 s</th><td>"Very strong" 40-year-old man</td><td>Expert target</td><td>Attia (The Drive #329)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">At least 90 s</th><td>"Very strong" 40-year-old woman</td><td>Expert target</td><td>Attia (The Drive #329)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">64.0 s</th><td>11 trained men with a mean age of about 20</td><td>Measured mean</td><td>GRIP-6 (2025)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">48.4 s</th><td>14 trained women with a mean age of about 20</td><td>Measured mean</td><td>GRIP-6 (2025)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>He also said to subtract about 10 seconds per decade. That is a rule of thumb from a podcast and not measured data. This page does not turn it into an age table. The wording comes from the episode transcript; the show notes are at peterattiamd.com. The 80:41 Guinness record is a separate category and is covered on the <a href="/dead-hang-world-record/">world record page</a>.</p>
<h3 id="published-targets">Published hang-time targets compared (none is a measured norm)</h3>
<p>Five publishers state hang-time levels. They disagree, and none is a measured population norm. Each row shows only what the source says, with the basis it gives. The measured figures are the GRIP-6 means above.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Hang-time levels published by five sources"><table><caption>Levels as each source names them, checked 1 November 2026. Seconds are as stated by the source. These are not measured norms.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Source and date</th><th scope="col">Who it is for</th><th scope="col">Middle level as named</th><th scope="col">Top level as named</th><th scope="col">Basis the source states</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.boxrox.com/how-long-should-you-be-able-to-dead-hang-based-on-your-age/" rel="noopener">BOXROX</a>, 25 July 2026</th><td>Ages 18 to 29</td><td>Average: 30 to 60 s</td><td>Excellent: over 90 s</td><td>"Practical performance targets based on research into grip strength, muscular endurance, healthy aging, and performance data from strength coaches." Also says there is "no official worldwide standard".</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">BOXROX, same article</th><td>Ages 40 to 49</td><td>Average: 20 to 40 s</td><td>Excellent: over 60 s</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.bodyspec.com/blog/post/dead_hang_benefits_form_and_training_plans" rel="noopener">BodySpec</a>, 29 July 2026</th><td>Ages 20 to 29</td><td>Intermediate: 45 s</td><td>Advanced: 90 s</td><td>"General guidance targets, not measured population norms."</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">BodySpec, same article</th><td>Ages 40 to 49</td><td>Intermediate: 35 s</td><td>Advanced: 70 s</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://marathonhandbook.com/good-hang-time/" rel="noopener">Marathon Handbook</a>, updated 7 May 2026</th><td>Fitness level, no age bands</td><td>Intermediate athletes: over 60 to 90 s</td><td>Advanced athletes: over 2 to 3 minutes</td><td>“There aren’t any official standards so we really can’t be exact.”</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.eatthis.com/dead-hang-grip-strength-test-ranking/" rel="noopener">Eat This</a>, 9 April 2025</th><td>Fitness level, no age bands</td><td>Intermediate: 21 to 45 s</td><td>Advanced: 46 to 90 s; Next-Level Grip: 91 s or more</td><td>No basis given on the page.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://theprehabguys.com/vimeo-video/dead-hang-assessment/" rel="noopener">The Prehab Guys</a>, updated 3 October 2023</th><td>Adults, no age bands</td><td>Good: 30 to 45 s</td><td>Extremely Good: 1:00 to 1:30; Elite: over 1:30</td><td>Listed as "Prehab Goals &amp; Normative Values". No source given.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Three sources use the label intermediate: Eat This means 21 to 45 s, BodySpec 45 s (ages 20 to 29) and Marathon Handbook over 60 to 90 s. None names a measured sample of people hanging as the basis for its bands. Use them to see the range of opinion and not to rank yourself.</p>
<h2 id="percentile-chart">Why we do not give dead-hang percentiles</h2>
<p>A percentile needs an appropriate comparison population and a standardized test. We do not have that dataset for these timed hangs. A result such as 60 seconds cannot truthfully become “top 25%” simply by assigning a band to it.</p>
<p>The calculator instead shows what fraction of your own target you have reached. A 30-second hold against a 60-second target is 50% of that target. It says nothing about how many people you outperform. The <a href="/dead-hang-standards/">dead hang standards page</a> covers how to make the test repeatable.</p>
<h2 id="is-my-time-good">Is your time useful to build from?</h2>
<p>A controlled hold gives you a starting record. A feet-supported hold is also recordable; keep it separate from an unassisted hold. Short holds are not failures. End the attempt while you can step down safely.</p>
<p>10, 30, 60 and 120 seconds are convenient round-number milestones. They are not validated age cut-offs or required fitness standards. Choose your next target and use the <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal hang calculator</a> to compare it. Set it as a goal in the <a href="/training-log/">training log</a>.</p>
<h3 id="what-does-your-time-mean">What does your time mean?</h3>
<p>A one-minute dead hang is a little under the 64-second mean of the men in the GRIP-6 study (11 men, mean age about 20) and above the 48-second mean of its women. It is well under Attia's two-minute target for a very strong 40-year-old man. Those are different groups. This is a rough comparison and not a ranking.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where common hang times sit against the sourced reference points"><table><caption>Where a hang time sits against the sourced reference points only. No percentiles.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Your time</th><th scope="col">Against the measured means (men 64.0 s, women 48.4 s)</th><th scope="col">Against Attia's targets (man 120 s, woman 90 s)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">10 to 20 s</th><td>Below both means</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">30 s</th><td>Below both means</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">45 s</th><td>Just under the women's mean and below the men's</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">60 s</th><td>Above the women's mean and just under the men's</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">90 s</th><td>Above both means</td><td>Meets the woman's target and below the man's</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">120 s</th><td>Above both means</td><td>Meets both targets</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">3 minutes or more</th><td>Above both means</td><td>Above both targets and far below the 80:41 record</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Individual times in the study varied by roughly 30 to 35 seconds (one standard deviation) either side of the means. A gap of 10 seconds says little. The calculator tracks progress against a target you choose. The <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang guide</a> covers short holds for people starting out.</p>
