<h2 id="age-decline">What changes when you repeat the test?</h2>
<p>Record your bar, grip and assistance consistently. A different bar diameter, chalk or foot support changes the conditions. Compare like with like. A lower result on one day is not a diagnosis of declining health. Heavier people generally hold less at the same grip strength, so compare with your own earlier holds.</p>
<h3 id="does-hang-time-fall-with-age">Does hang time fall with age?</h3>
<p>Grip force falls with age. Nobody has measured how many hanging seconds are lost per decade.</p><p>A pooled analysis of 12 British studies (60,803 measurements from 49,964 participants) found median peak grip of 51 kg in males aged 29 to 39 and 31 kg in females aged 26 to 42 (Dodds et al. PLOS ONE 2014). That is force in kilograms and not seconds. The <a href="/grip-strength-by-age/">grip strength by age guide</a> covers the force side.</p>
<h2 id="improve">Turn your baseline into a routine</h2>
<p>Choose manageable holds and enough rest to maintain control. Record what you actually held instead of saving the timer setting as a result.</p><p>The training plans organize practice and review points without promising a particular gain.</p>
<h2 id="how-long-per-session">How long should you dead hang in a session?</h2>
<p>Level 1 is the starting dose: 3 hangs of 15 seconds each, with 90 seconds of rest between hangs, on 3 non-consecutive days a week (for example Monday, Wednesday and Friday). Stop each hang 1 to 2 seconds before your grip gives out. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription. Stop if you feel sharp pain. This is not medical advice.</p>
<p>Rest, progression and how to add sessions are covered in <a href="/deadhang-frequency/">how often to dead hang</a>.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Common questions about hang time</h2>{allFaqs.map(([q,a,href])=><details class="answer"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}{href && <> The full dose is in <a href={href}>the session section above</a>.</>}</p></details>)}
<h2 id="sources">Sources and method</h2><p>The hang times come from the GRIP-6 study (Chomiuk et al. 2025). Dodds et al. (2014) supplies the grip-force figures. They measure squeeze force in kilograms and not hanging time. The personal-goal examples on this page are arithmetic and editorial milestones and not research-derived training prescriptions.</p><p>The <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a> explain how we distinguish research and interpretation from equipment advice.</p>
<div class="evidence-note"><p><strong>Benchmark correction, 11 September 2026.</strong> Earlier versions of this page presented age-specific seconds and percentiles as established norms. We could not substantiate those claims from the cited sources. They have been removed. This page now separates published grip-strength evidence from personal training goals.</p></div>

